{
  "root_cause": "Escape keydown triggers the dropdown's hide() method, which correctly closes the menu and updates aria-expanded but does not restore focus to the toggle button, leaving focus on the now-hidden menu item or causing it to revert to the body.",
  "repair_strategy": "Modify the hide() method in dropdown.js to return focus to the toggle element after completing the hide operation, ensuring that closing the menu via Escape (or any other means) restores keyboard focus to the button that opened it.",
  "edits": [
    {
      "path": "js/src/dropdown.js",
      "sha256": "fc69a61b69ae1fa2c2bb923fdc393dd2742925e8af996376df5f98e38f8d810e",
      "before": "  hide() {\n    if (isDisabled(this._element) || !this._isShown(this._menu)) {\n      return;\n    }\n\n    const relatedTarget = {\n      relatedTarget: this._element\n    };\n\n    this._completeHide(relatedTarget);\n  }",
      "after": "  hide() {\n    if (isDisabled(this._element) || !this._isShown(this._menu)) {\n      return;\n    }\n\n    const relatedTarget = {\n      relatedTarget: this._element\n    };\n\n    this._completeHide(relatedTarget);\n    this._element.focus();\n  }"
  }
},
  "expected_accessibility_effect": "Pressing Escape while the actions menu is open will close the menu and move focus back to the 'Invoice actions' toggle button, satisfying keyboard accessibility requirements for dismissing popups.",
  "functional_risks": [
    "Calling hide() when the toggle is already focused will refocus it, which is harmless."
  ],
  "verification_requirements": [
    "After Escape keydown on a menu item, focus must be on #actions-toggle and aria-expanded='false'.",
    "The #actions-menu must not have the 'show' class (i.e., be hidden).",
    "Subsequent ArrowDown keydown on #actions-toggle must open the menu and focus the first menu item (#action-send)."
  ]
}

reasoning:
We need to analyze the problem.

The journey steps:

Step 0: seek #actions-toggle

Step 1: press ArrowDown -> expects attribute aria-expanded="true" on #actions-toggle and focus moves to #action-send

Step 2: press Escape -> expects attribute aria-expanded="false" on #actions-toggle and focus back to #actions-toggle

Observed failure at step 2: After pressing Escape, they got focus still on something else? Let's look at observed failed step details.

In "observed.failedSteps", index 2 corresponds to Escape press.

They had after: shows the whole body HTML where the menu is closed (aria-expanded false) and visible etc. They assert two things:

Assertion 1: kind attribute name aria-expanded selector #actions-toggle value false -> observed false pass true (so attribute correct)

Assertion 2: kind focus selector #actions-toggle -> observed false pass false (meaning focus not on #actions-toggle).

Thus after Escape, focus didn't return to toggle; instead remained somewhere else (maybe on body?). In the after snapshot, focus is on body (as shown by state.focus selector body). So focus ended up on body.

Goal: When Escape pressed, focus should return to toggle (#actions-toggle). Currently, the dropdown.js likely handles Escape via dataApiKeydownHandler which calls instance.hide() when Escape event occurs on the toggle or menu? Let's examine.

In dropdown.js, there is static method dataApiKeydownHandler(event). It handles keydown events on SELECTOR_DATA_TOGGLE (the toggle) and SELECTOR_MENU (the menu). For Escape key, if isEscapeEvent and not isInput, then:

if (isEscapeEvent) {
  instance.hide()
  return
}

But note: This handler is attached to document for keydown events on SELECTOR_DATA_TOGGLE and SELECTOR_MENU.

When focus is on a menu item (like #action-send) and Escape is pressed, the event target is that button (which is inside the menu). The selector SELECTOR_MENU matches the ul.dropdown-menu, not the button inside. However, the event handler is attached to document with selector SELECTOR_MENU, meaning it will fire when the event target matches SELECTOR_MENU or is a descendant? Actually, EventHandler.on uses delegated listener: the second argument is event type, third is selector, fourth is handler. So it sets up a listener on the document (first argument) for keydown events, and when event occurs, it checks if event.target matches the selector (or is a descendant?). In Bootstrap's EventHandler.on, they use delegate: they check if event.target matches selector? I think they use delegated handling: they attach listener to element, and when event bubbles, they check if event.target matches selector. So for SELECTOR_MENU, it will match when the target is the ul.dropdown-menu or any of its descendants? Usually delegated listener matches if target matches selector; but they might use element.matches(selector) check. Not sure. But likely they only fire when the target itself matches the selector, not descendants. However, they also have a separate handler for SELECTOR_DATA_TOGGLE.

Thus when focus is on a button inside the menu, Escape keydown may not trigger the dataApiKeydownHandler because target is button, not ul or toggle. Then the event may bubble up and be handled by something else? There's also a global keyup listener for clearMenus on keyup? Actually they have:

EventHandler.on(document, EVENT_KEYUP_DATA_API, Dropdown.clearMenus)

So on keyup (not keydown) they call clearMenus. That may cause menu to hide but not manage focus.

Our failure is on keydown Escape (they captured keyEvents: key Escape, target #action-send, type keydown, defaultPrevented:true). So the keydown event was prevented default (by something). Likely the dataApiKeydownHandler on SELECTOR_MENU didn't fire, but maybe some other listener prevented default and stopped propagation? Actually they show defaultPrevented:true for Escape keydown on #action-send. That suggests some listener called preventDefault() on that event. Which listener? Possibly the dropdown's dataApiKeydownHandler on SELECTOR_MENU? But if it didn't match, maybe they have a listener on the button itself? Not in code.

Let's examine the clearMenus method: It is called on click and keyup events. It checks if event.button === RIGHT_MOUSE_BUTTON or (event.type === 'keyup' && event.key !== TAB_KEY) then return. So on keyup, if key is not Tab, it returns early. For Escape keyup, event.type === 'keyup' and event.key !== TAB_KEY (Escape != Tab) so it returns early, thus clearMenus not run on Escape keyup. So Escape keyup does nothing.

Thus Escape keydown must be handled elsewhere. Perhaps the browser's default behavior for Escape when focus is inside a dropdown menu is to close it and return focus to the toggle? Actually native behavior: pressing Escape while focus is inside a custom dropdown (non-native) may not move focus. Many libraries handle it.

In Bootstrap's dropdown.js, they handle Escape via dataApiKeydownHandler on both toggle and menu. But as argued, the menu selector may not capture descendants. Let's verify: In Bootstrap v5, the dataApiKeydownHandler is attached to document with selector SELECTOR_DATA_TOGGLE and SELECTOR_MENU. According to Bootstrap source, they indeed listen on the document for those selectors, and the handler checks if the target matches the selector (or is a child?). I recall that they use SelectorEngine.find? Actually they use event delegation: they attach listener to document, and in handler they get the toggle button via this.matches(SELECTOR_DATA_TOGGLE) ? this : SelectorEngine.prev(this, SELECTOR_DATA_TOGGLE)[0]; So they expect that 'this' (the event target) is either the toggle or an element inside the menu (since they call SelectorEngine.prev to find the toggle). Wait: Look at the code:

const getToggleButton = this.matches(SELECTOR_DATA_TOGGLE) ? this : SelectorEngine.prev(this, SELECTOR_DATA_TOGGLE)[0]

Thus they assume that 'this' (event.target) is either the toggle or an element inside the menu (since they look for previous toggle). So they rely on the event target being inside the menu (like a button) to still find the toggle via previous sibling? Actually SelectorEngine.prev(this, SELECTOR_DATA_TOGGLE) looks for previous sibling matching selector, not ancestor. That seems wrong. But maybe they intend that when focus is on a menu item, the previous sibling is the toggle? No, the toggle is before the ul, not sibling of li. Hmm.

Wait: The markup: button#actions-toggle, then ul#actions-menu. The li buttons are inside the ul. So the previous sibling of a li button is the previous li or maybe the ul? Not the toggle.

Thus the logic may be flawed for nested structure. However, Bootstrap's dropdown works; maybe they rely on the fact that the menu items are direct children of the ul, and the ul is next sibling of the toggle. So to get toggle from a menu item, you go up to parentNode (the ul), then previous sibling is the toggle. But they used SelectorEngine.prev which looks at previousElementSibling, not parent. So maybe they rely on the event being triggered on the ul itself (the menu) when navigating with arrow keys? Actually when you press ArrowDown, focus moves to first item, but the keydown event target is the toggle? Let's see: In dataApiKeydownHandler, when isUpOrDownEvent, they call instance.show() and instance._selectMenuItem(event). The _selectMenuItem focuses an item. The keydown event that triggered show is still on the toggle (since keydown on toggle). After showing, they call _selectMenuItem which focuses the item, but the keydown event is already processed.

Now for Escape: When focus is on a menu item, keydown Escape target is the menu item (button). The handler will get this = button. this.matches(SELECTOR_DATA_TOGGLE) false, so they go to SelectorEngine.prev(this, SELECTOR_DATA_TOGGLE)[0] which looks for previous sibling with data-bs-toggle="dropdown". Since the button's previous sibling is maybe another li or none, they won't find toggle, so getToggleButton returns null? Actually SelectorEngine.prev returns array; they take [0]; if empty, undefined. Then instance = Dropdown.getOrCreateInstance(getToggleButton) would be called with undefined, likely returns null? Then they'd have error? But they have a guard: if (isDisabled(this)) return; but not for null toggle. Might cause error silently? However, they observed defaultPrevented:true, meaning preventDefault was called. That likely came from the handler calling event.preventDefault()? Let's see: In dataApiKeydownHandler, after checking isEscapeEvent, they call instance.hide() and return. They do NOT call event.preventDefault() or stopPropagation() for Escape. Wait they do: At top they have:

if (!isInput && !(isUpOrDownEvent || isEscapeEvent)) {
  return;
}

if (isInput && !isEscapeEvent) {
  // ...
  return;
}

const isActive = this.classList.contains(CLASS_NAME_SHOW)

if (!isActive && isEscapeEvent) {
  return;
}

event.preventDefault()
event.stopPropagation()

So for Escape, they always call preventDefault and stopPropagation (unless early return). Indeed they call preventDefault and stopPropagation for Escape regardless of isActive? Actually they have:

if (!isActive && isEscapeEvent) {
  return;
}

So if not active and Escape, they return early (no preventDefault). If active (menu shown) and Escape, they continue, preventDefault and stopPropagation, then getToggleButton and instance.hide().

Thus when menu is shown (active true) and Escape keydown on a menu item, they will preventDefault and stopPropagation, then try to hide.

Thus observed defaultPrevented:true matches that.

Now after hide(), focus should return to toggle. The hide method calls _completeHide which removes SHOW class, sets aria-expanded false, etc. It does not manage focus. The show method sets focus on toggle: this._element.focus(); this._element.setAttribute('aria-expanded', true). Hide does not restore focus.

Thus after Escape, focus remains where it was (on the menu item) because hide doesn't move focus. However, after hide, the menu is hidden, but focus remains on the now-hidden button (which may be inaccessible or not visible). In observed state, focus ended up on body (maybe because the button is hidden/display none? Actually the button is still in DOM but its parent ul has no show class, but it's still visible? The ul is not shown (no class show) but still present; however the button inside may still be focusable but maybe not visible due to being hidden? The ul likely has display: block when show, otherwise maybe display: none? Actually .dropdown-menu is positioned absolute and shown via class show which adds display: block. Without show, it's still present but maybe not displayed (due to being positioned off-screen?). In any case, the button may be hidden (not visible) causing browser to move focus to body? Or the focus stays on the button but it's not visible, causing test to consider focus not on toggle.

Thus we need to modify the hide method to return focus to the toggle when hiding via Escape (or generally when hiding). According to ARIA authoring practices, when a menu is closed, focus should return to the button that opened it.

Thus we should adjust the hide method (or _completeHide) to focus the toggle after hiding, if the hide was triggered from Escape? But we cannot differentiate cause. Simpler: always focus toggle after hide. However, show already focuses toggle; hide should return focus.

But we must ensure that focusing toggle after hide does not interfere when hide is called programmatically (e.g., clicking outside). That's still desired: clicking outside should also return focus to toggle. So it's fine.

Thus edit dropdown.js hide method to add focus toggle after hiding.

Specifically, in hide() method after _completeHide(relatedTarget), we can add this._element.focus();

But need to ensure we don't break if element is disabled? Already checked earlier.

